* AD8421 instrumentation amplifier (U27), behavioral, written for this
* project. Not the model of the manufacturer.
* Analog Devices datasheet AD8421 Rev. A; the A grade (AD8421ARZ), typical
* at 25 C unless a limit is named.
*   Gain: G = 1 + 9.9 kohm / RG (pages 4 and 22). The model has the two
*   4.95 kohm feedback resistors of figure 62 (page 22) and takes the gain
*   resistor from the circuit. Gain error of the part 0.2 % at most for
*   G = 10 to 1000 (page 4): the parameter gerr scales the two resistors,
*   0 by default.
*   Small-signal bandwidth 10 MHz at G = 1 and G = 10, 2 MHz at G = 100,
*   0.2 MHz at G = 1000 (page 4): a first stage with a gain-bandwidth
*   product of 200 MHz (gbwi) and an output stage with one pole at 10 MHz
*   (fout). That gives 10, 8.4, 1.9 and 0.2 MHz, and 6.4 MHz at G = 19.93.
*   Slew rate 35 V/us (page 4): sr, a limit of the output stage.
*   Voltage noise at 1 kHz: 3 nV/rtHz of the input stage, 3.2 at most, and
*   60 nV/rtHz of the output stage (page 3; figure 42 on page 18 shows 60
*   as the typical level at G = 1): eni and eno. The 1/f corners, 1.5 Hz
*   (fci) and 5 Hz (fco), are fitted to 0.07 uV and 2 uV peak to peak from
*   0.1 Hz to 10 Hz (page 3) and to figure 42; the model gives 0.08 uV and
*   2.3 uV.
*   Current noise 200 fA/rtHz at 1 kHz and 18 pA peak to peak from 0.1 Hz
*   to 10 Hz (page 3): inz, with a corner of 40 Hz (fcn) that also follows
*   figure 44 (page 18).
*   Offset: 60 uV of the input stage and 350 uV of the output stage at
*   most, of either sign (page 3): vosi and voso, 0 by default. The two
*   add as input offset times gain plus output offset (page 5). The sign
*   of vosi is the one of the first version of this file and of vos in
*   opamps.lib: a positive vosi lowers the output by the gain times vosi.
*   A positive voso raises the output by voso.
*   Bias current 1 nA typical, 2 nA at most; offset current 0.5 nA typical,
*   2 nA at most (page 3): ib is the current into each input and ios the
*   difference between the two, both 0 by default.
*   Input impedance 30 Gohm and 3 pF, between the inputs and from each
*   input (page 4). The 30 Gohm from each input ends on the middle of the
*   supply, which gives the slope of figure 19 (page 14).
*   Common-mode rejection from DC to 60 Hz at the least: 86 dB at G = 1,
*   106 dB at G = 10, 126 dB at G = 100; 80 dB at 20 kHz and G = 1
*   (page 3). The error is made in the output stage, so it falls with the
*   gain as the table says. cmrr is the rejection at G = 1 in dB, 200 by
*   default (none): the table gives a limit and no typical value. cmsign
*   (1 or -1) is the sign of the error, fcmr the frequency above which it
*   rises, 11.5 kHz from the two figures for G = 1.
*   Limits of the first stage: its two outputs sit one level shift (vbe)
*   below the inputs, moved apart by half the output signal, and may come
*   no closer than hlo to the negative and hhi to the positive supply. The
*   manufacturer's range tool states 1.4 V, 1.8 V and a shift of 0.58 V;
*   the model uses a shift of 0.40 V, which gives the 20 uA of the
*   reference pin (page 4), and hlo = 1.58 V, hhi = 1.62 V, so that the
*   sums are those of the tool. Figures 11 and 13 (page 13) agree with
*   them. Figure 14 (G = 100 at +/-5 V) shows less room near the negative
*   supply; hlo = 2.38 V reproduces it near an output of 2.5 V. Beyond a
*   limit one half of the first stage stops and the gain falls to about 2.
*   Output swing: -Vs + 1.2 V to +Vs - 1.6 V into 2 kohm are the limits
*   of page 4; figure 36 (page 17) shows -Vs + 1.1 V and +Vs - 1.4 V at
*   2 kohm and 25 C, the values of vlo and vhi.
*   Short-circuit current 65 mA (page 4): isc.
*   Reference pin: 20 kohm to the first stage (page 4).
*   Input current in overdrive: figures 17 and 18 (page 14) show, at
*   G = 100, no visible current up to about 2 V between the inputs, then
*   about 3 mA/V, bending over near 10 mA. The model lets a current pass
*   from an input into its gain pin when the pin is more than vfwd below
*   it, and from a gain pin into its input when the pin is more than vrev
*   above it, each through rod. The values are a fit to that one figure
*   up to 5 mA; with another gain resistor the path is an assumption.
*   Output resistance in closed loop 0.05 ohm (ro): an assumption, the
*   datasheet gives none.
*   Supply current 2 mA, 2.3 mA at most (page 5): iq.
* What it leaves out: the peaking of the response near 8 MHz (figure 22),
* the fall of the rejection with frequency at gains above 1 (90 dB at the
* least at 20 kHz and G = 10, where the model keeps 100 dB), the upper end
* of the input range at low gain (+Vs - 1.8 V in the table; the model
* limits at +Vs - 1.2 V), the overload recovery time (not in the
* datasheet: the model recovers as fast as it slews), supply rejection,
* gain nonlinearity, drift, the bend of the overdrive current near 10 mA,
* phase inversion under extreme overdrive.
* Pins in the order of the package: -IN RG RG +IN -VS REF OUT +VS.

* Unit noise sources: white 1 nV/rtHz and 1/f with 1 uV/rtHz at 1 Hz, as
* the open-circuit voltage of resistors (see OPAMP_UNITNOISE in opamps.lib).
.subckt AD8421_UNITNOISE nw nf
Rw nw 0 60.328
R1 nf n1 8.8434e9
C1 nf n1 1.7997n
R2 n1 n2 8.8434e8
C2 n1 n2 1.7997n
R3 n2 n3 8.8434e7
C3 n2 n3 1.7997n
R4 n3 n4 8.8434e6
C4 n3 n4 1.7997n
R5 n4 n5 8.8434e5
C5 n4 n5 1.7997n
R6 n5 n6 8.8434e4
C6 n5 n6 1.7997n
R7 n6 n7 8.8434e3
C7 n6 n7 1.7997n
R8 n7 0 8.8434e2
C8 n7 0 1.7997n
.ends AD8421_UNITNOISE

* Supply present: 1 above 4 V between the supply pins, 0 below.
.func ad8421_alive(vs) {0.5*(1 + tanh((vs - 4)/0.3))}
.subckt AD8421 inn rga rgb inp vn ref out vp
+ params: vosi=0 voso=0 ib=0 cmrr=200 gbwi=200meg fout=10meg sr=35meg
+ vhi=1.4 vlo=1.1 ios=0 gerr=0 eni=3n fci=1.5 eno=60n fco=5 inz=200f
+ fcn=40 cmsign=1 fcmr=11.5k vbe=0.4 hlo=1.58 hhi=1.62 vfwd=0.7 vrev=1.5
+ rod=125 isc=65m iq=2m ro=0.05
.param kcm={cmsign*exp(-cmrr*0.11512925)}
.param tcm={1/(6.2832*fcmr*1n)}
.param kwo={eno/1n}
.param kfo={eno*sqrt(fco)/1u}
* inputs: impedance and bias current
Bmid mid 0 V = 0.5*(v(vp) + v(vn))
Rca inn mid 30g
Rcb inp mid 30g
Rd inp inn 30g
Cca inn vn 3p
Ccb inp vn 3p
Cd inp inn 3p
Iba inn vn {ib - ios/2}
Ibb inp vn {ib + ios/2}
* voltage noise of the input stage, half in each input, and the offset
Xna nwa nfa AD8421_UNITNOISE
Xnb nwb nfb AD8421_UNITNOISE
Ewa inn pa1 nwa 0 {eni/1.4142/1n}
Efa pa1 pa nfa 0 {eni/1.4142*sqrt(fci)/1u}
Ewb inp pb1 nwb 0 {eni/1.4142/1n}
Efb pb1 pb2 nfb 0 {eni/1.4142*sqrt(fci)/1u}
Vosi pb2 pb {vosi}
* current noise of each input
Xnc nwc nfc AD8421_UNITNOISE
Xnd nwd nfd AD8421_UNITNOISE
Gwa inn vn nwc 0 {inz/1n}
Gfa inn vn nfc 0 {inz*sqrt(fcn)/1u}
Gwb inp vn nwd 0 {inz/1n}
Gfb inp vn nfd 0 {inz*sqrt(fcn)/1u}
* first stage: each half holds its gain pin one level shift below its
* input and drives it through 4.95 kohm; its output stops at the limits.
* The limits are written so that they cannot cross at the first iteration
* of an operating point, when every node is still at 0 V. The integrators
* work on 10 pF: with a larger capacitor their transconductance turns the
* rounding of the solver into currents that it takes for an error.
Bga 0 xa I = {6.2832*gbwi*10p}*(v(pa) - {vbe} - v(rga))
Cxa xa 0 10p
Rxa xa 0 1e9
Bwa xa 0 I = 0.1*(max(v(xa) - max(v(vp) - {hhi}, 0.5*(v(vp) + v(vn)) + 0.5) - 0.02, 0) + min(v(xa) - min(v(vn) + {hlo}, 0.5*(v(vp) + v(vn)) - 0.5) + 0.02, 0))
Bya ya 0 V = min(max(v(xa), min(v(vn) + {hlo}, 0.5*(v(vp) + v(vn)) - 0.5)), max(v(vp) - {hhi}, 0.5*(v(vp) + v(vn)) + 0.5))
Rfa ya rga {4950*(1 + gerr)} noisy=0
Bgb 0 xb I = {6.2832*gbwi*10p}*(v(pb) - {vbe} - v(rgb))
Cxb xb 0 10p
Rxb xb 0 1e9
Bwb xb 0 I = 0.1*(max(v(xb) - max(v(vp) - {hhi}, 0.5*(v(vp) + v(vn)) + 0.5) - 0.02, 0) + min(v(xb) - min(v(vn) + {hlo}, 0.5*(v(vp) + v(vn)) - 0.5) + 0.02, 0))
Byb yb 0 V = min(max(v(xb), min(v(vn) + {hlo}, 0.5*(v(vp) + v(vn)) - 0.5)), max(v(vp) - {hhi}, 0.5*(v(vp) + v(vn)) + 0.5))
Rfb yb rgb {4950*(1 + gerr)} noisy=0
* input current in overdrive, through the gain pins. The two resistors of
* 1 Tohm keep the same places of the solver's matrix in use whether a path
* conducts or not; without them some operating points are not found.
Roa inn rga 1e12 noisy=0
Rob inp rgb 1e12 noisy=0
Bfa inn rga I = max(v(inn) - v(rga) - {vfwd}, 0)/{rod}
Bra rga inn I = max(v(rga) - v(inn) - {vrev}, 0)/{rod}
Bfb inp rgb I = max(v(inp) - v(rgb) - {vfwd}, 0)/{rod}
Brb rgb inp I = max(v(rgb) - v(inp) - {vrev}, 0)/{rod}
* output stage: difference of the two halves, referred to the REF pin
* through 10 kohm and 10 kohm; the common-mode error is made here
Rda yb pd 10k noisy=0
Rdb pd ref 10k noisy=0
Bcmn cmn 0 V = 0.5*(v(ya) + v(yb)) + {vbe}
Ccm cmn cm1 1n
Rcm cm1 cm2 159 noisy=0
Vcm cm2 0 0
Xno nwo nfo AD8421_UNITNOISE
Bt t 0 V = 2*v(pd) - v(ya) + {voso} + {kcm}*(v(cmn) + {tcm}*i(Vcm)) + {kwo}*v(nwo) + {kfo}*v(nfo)
* the target is bounded by the output swing; the output follows it at the
* slew rate. At DC the inductor ties the two, so that an operating
* point does not depend on the slew limiter.
Btc tc 0 V = min(max(v(t), min(v(vn) + {vlo}, 0.5*(v(vp) + v(vn)) - 0.5)), max(v(vp) - {vhi}, 0.5*(v(vp) + v(vn)) + 0.5))
Bs 0 s I = max(min({6.2832*fout*1n}*v(tc,s), {sr*1n}), {-sr*1n})
Ls tc s 100m
Cs s 0 1n
Rs s 0 1e9
Bo o 0 V = ad8421_alive(v(vp,vn))*v(s) + (1 - ad8421_alive(v(vp,vn)))*v(vn)
Bio io 0 V = max(min(v(o,out)/{ro}, {isc}), {-isc})
Bout 0 out I = v(io)
Rol o out 1k noisy=0
* supply current: the quiescent current and the current of the load
Bqp vp 0 I = ad8421_alive(v(vp,vn))*{iq} + max(v(io), 0)
Bqn 0 vn I = ad8421_alive(v(vp,vn))*{iq} + max(-v(io), 0)
.ends AD8421
